{
  "title": "Remove the legacy last_sync_at path from shopping-list sync",
  "description": "POST /api/shopping-list/sync now cursors on `change_xid` (`cursor` in request and response), but still accepts `last_sync_at` and filters on wall-clock `updated_at` when `cursor` is absent (`ChangesSince::Timestamp` in server/src/api/shopping_list/sync.rs). \
That path keeps the commit-race that the cursor fixed, and exists only for iOS builds released before the cursor; current iOS sends `lastSyncAt` only when it has no stored cursor (first sync after upgrading), and web never sends it. \
Once pre-cursor iOS builds are no longer in use, drop `last_sync_at` from SyncRequest, the Timestamp branch, the deprecated `sync_timestamp` cursor docs, and probably the `(user_id, updated_at)` index `idx_shopping_list_sync` that only that branch uses. iOS still uses the stored sync time for its 5-minute staleness check, so keep storing it locally (it can come from the local clock instead of `sync_timestamp`).",
  "status": "open",
  "blocked": "Pre-cursor iOS builds must age out first. The xid cursor shipped 2026-10-10 (eaca115c9, c2807bf17); until then, iOS builds without it rely on `last_sync_at`, and dropping it would make them pull the full list and miss deletions. Unblock once every installed iOS build sends `cursor`.",
  "priority": 4,
